1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:07,440
This is the Pi Traffic Tester, running on a Raspberry Pi.

2
00:00:07,690 --> 00:00:11,370
The lamps along the top show this Pi's health at a glance.

3
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:18,440
The other Pi answers, the clocks agree, and the network port is up.

4
00:00:21,870 --> 00:00:26,700
The Links table lists every port, and whether the far Pi answers on it.

5
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:31,270
Below it is the test that is loaded, ready to start.

6
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:40,570
And the latest results are always on the right.

7
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:44,990
Open Tests to choose what to run.

8
00:00:45,270 --> 00:00:51,250
Tests are grouped: trip signals, whole-network test sets, and basic link checks.

9
00:00:54,550 --> 00:01:00,030
Tap a card's information link to read, in plain words, what the test does.

10
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:04,700
It explains how it works, what you need, and what a pass means.

11
00:01:07,620 --> 00:01:10,680
To build your own test, press New test.

12
00:01:11,620 --> 00:01:15,060
Answer four short rows, from top to bottom.

13
00:01:15,840 --> 00:01:20,380
Port: where the traffic leaves from. Here, the Pi's Wi-Fi.

14
00:01:20,630 --> 00:01:23,730
Protocol: plain UDP messages.

15
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:30,760
From and To: pi1 sends, pi2 answers, and each trip is timed there and back.

16
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:37,790
The app warns that this path shares Wi-Fi, so the results include Wi-Fi delay. That is fine for a demo.

17
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:43,020
Traffic: a message every 5 milliseconds, for 30 seconds.

18
00:01:46,310 --> 00:01:51,740
Pass means 99 percent of round trips arrive within 60 milliseconds.

19
00:01:51,990 --> 00:01:56,170
Give it a name and press Save, and it joins your own tests.

20
00:02:02,340 --> 00:02:04,410
Press Run test.

21
00:02:06,520 --> 00:02:09,550
The Pi checks the path, then starts sending.

22
00:02:09,800 --> 00:02:19,940
The live numbers update every second: delay now, the slowest one percent, the slowest ever, messages lost, and messages over the limit.

23
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:31,200
The arrows show each request going out, and each reply coming back.

24
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:39,060
The chart plots the delay of every message against your limit line.

25
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:42,840
When the time is up, the verdict appears.

26
00:02:44,770 --> 00:02:48,760
Open Results to see every run, newest first.

27
00:02:49,020 --> 00:02:54,020
PASS means the link met every requirement. FAIL tells you which one it missed.

28
00:02:54,280 --> 00:03:03,170
The red note is the Pi checking itself. It paused briefly while sending, and says so, so you can tell a Pi limit from a link problem.

29
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:12,500
Below are the numbers: the typical delay, the 95th and 99th percentiles, the slowest message, jitter and loss.

30
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:16,490
The chart shows how the delay was spread across the run.

31
00:03:18,870 --> 00:03:25,290
The PDF report packs the verdict and the numbers, with a sign-off sheet, ready to hand over.

32
00:03:27,040 --> 00:03:31,100
Saved tests live under My tests, at the bottom of the list.

33
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,760
To run one again, press Run, then Run test.

34
00:03:41,830 --> 00:03:46,180
Every run is stored, so you can repeat it after you change the link.

35
00:03:47,940 --> 00:03:52,630
Did anything change? Back in Results, choose Select to compare.

36
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:56,490
Tick two runs of the same test, and press Compare.

37
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:08,240
Each number is shown side by side, with the change, and whether it is better or worse.

38
00:04:08,490 --> 00:04:14,040
Set one run as the baseline, and every later run of that test is shown against it.

39
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:20,540
The Sniffer captures packets from the wire, like a protocol analyser.

40
00:04:20,790 --> 00:04:24,880
Pick where it listens. Here, a mirror port on the Ethernet.

41
00:04:25,130 --> 00:04:30,830
Press Start, and packets appear as they arrive. The other Pi is sending test traffic.

42
00:04:39,080 --> 00:04:44,100
Press Stop to keep the capture. Save it as pcapng, or reopen it here later.

43
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:53,300
Tap a packet to decode it, layer by layer, with its bytes.

44
00:04:54,720 --> 00:05:01,060
Type a display filter, such as port 20500, to show only what matters.

45
00:05:02,810 --> 00:05:05,000
Every result can be exported.

46
00:05:05,250 --> 00:05:12,460
Use the PDF report for a customer, the ZIP for the raw data, and CSV or JSON for your own tools.

47
00:05:14,610 --> 00:05:22,390
That is the whole loop: set up a test, run it, read the verdict, compare, and capture. Thanks for watching.

